Phase 3 — README.md
Phase 3 — Execution & Authorization

Phase 3 is where hands start moving.
This is where execution becomes legally possible — if, and only if, a Phase 3 Execution License has been purchased.

Until that happens, this material is not permission to build, prototype, deploy, integrate, submit to contracts, or operationalize in any form. If you are not licensed, stop here.

Purpose of Phase 3

Phase 3 exists to:

authorize execution

prevent concept drift during execution

tie work to the validated Phase 0 / Phase 1 / Phase 2 intent

ensure nobody freelances the architecture into stupidity

This is where you move from:

“We understand it”
to
“We’re going to actually do it — for real.”

Legal Status

Phase 3 content is governed by the Execution License, not the Theory License.
If you do not have execution rights, you do not get to touch hardware, software, or implementation.

Phase 3 = Paid, Licensed Execution Zone.

Execution License Grants

Once a valid Phase 3 Execution License is obtained, the licensee is permitted to:

build prototypes

conduct lab testing

conduct field testing

integrate into programs

submit to contracts

operationally deploy in limited or full capacity

Execution must remain consistent with the documented architecture. “Execute honestly or don’t execute at all.”

What is NOT Allowed Without a License

If you do NOT have a license, you cannot:

build anything

bench test

field test

integrate into systems

deploy

submit to NASA, DoD, SBIR, DARPA, ESA, JAXA, or any other government contract

include in a research program

perform “just testing”

run internal pilots

build student / academic prototypes

“tinker to see if it works”

If hands touch reality, it’s execution.
Execution requires a license.

Commercialization Definition

These count as Commercialization / Execution and require a license:

submitting in an RFP / proposal

SBIR / government / defense submission

academic grant proposals involving build or testing

corporate internal development

startup R&D

integration into existing products

prototyping with intent to use

field demonstration

use in funded or unfunded programs

If it moves beyond thought, it’s execution.

What Does NOT Count as Commercialization

These do NOT require a license:

reading

academic discussion

internal design debate

analysis / critique

simulation work (non-executable, no prototype fabrication, no field use)

planning / feasibility reviews

paper studies

Thinking is free.
Doing is licensed.

No Warranty

Execution is at your risk.
No guarantees of correctness, performance, safety, or suitability.
Reality will test you whether you respect it or not.
